# config/kernel/Kconfig.energy — Energy accounting and thermal management

config INFSRV_ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ
    int "Inference service: energy quota of a context per window (uJ)"
    range 0 2000000000
    default 0
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ: the energy one context may
      spend per window on inference, microjoules. A variant is admitted only
      when its signed energy fits what is left of the window (Rule
      accel-admission, refused with a receipt otherwise), and a run that
      overran its signed worst case is charged for the overrun when it
      finishes, so the next call sees less. 0: no quota.

config INFSRV_ENERGY_WINDOW_MS
    int "Inference service: energy quota window (ms)"
    range 1 3600000
    default 1000
    help
      userspace/services/infsrv, ENERGY_WINDOW_MS: the quota above is spent
      afresh every window of this length, counted from a context's first
      call after the last window ended. Unused with INFSRV_ENERGY_QUOTA_UJ 0.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Energy-aware scheduling (stages E0-E4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config ENERGY
    # cargo-feature: energy
    bool "Energy-aware scheduling: utilisation tracking and the energy model"
    default y if PRESET_WEARABLE || PRESET_IOT_HMI || PRESET_DESKTOP || PRESET_SERVER
    help
      Energy-aware scheduling. Emits the kernel cargo feature `energy`
      (tools/build/kconfig_to_cargo.py).

      The seams themselves (Placement, Governor, IdleGovernor in
      crates/core/energy) are in every build with today's behaviour only:
      Legacy wake-up placement, no frequency request (the boot clock), `wfi`
      in both idle tasks. With this option off they fold to the code they
      replaced: the kernel's .text is unchanged (invariant I4).

      y = also track per-task and per-CPU utilisation (ENERGY_UTIL, updated at
        every dispatch, switch-out, wake-up and tick), and read the energy
        model at boot: the signed topology's `[energy.domain.*]` sections,
        else the DTB's operating-points-v2 / idle-states, else (x86_64
        hybrid parts) one capacity class per core type the CPUs report
        through CPUID (Intel P/E, AMD Zen 5/5c), else (Raspberry Pi: the
        mailbox is the board object) one domain from the
        firmware's ARM clock range with placeholder, unmeasured power, else
        none. The
        model is validated and reported (`[ENERGY]` lines); an invalid one is
        refused and the machine runs with none. The E3 frequency governor
        (Schedutil, DeadlineFloor) and the E4 idle governor run only when the
        topology carries a model and mode `balanced` or `endurance`. The
        frequency governor decides in the tick and the board's apply step
        moves the clock, with or without THERMAL: the kernel's `thermal`
        task (kernel/src/boot/thermal.rs), built whenever the board has a
        clock to move (the JH7110's CPU clock with SOC_FRAMEWORKS, the K1's
        clusters with SOC_K1, the Raspberry Pi's ARM clock through the
        mailbox, the x86 HWP/CPPC level request), woken when a choice
        changes. It answers every choice, and the governor asserts the
        answer comes within ENERGY_APPLY_DEADLINE_MS: a refused or overdue
        choice is counted (`POLICY_STATS_OPP_*`) and recorded (`opp-apply`).
        A board with no actuator (QEMU) builds no apply step: the decisions
        are counted and nothing is asserted. The idle governor enters the
        state it picks (PSCI CPU_SUSPEND, SBI hart_suspend, x86 mwait; `wfi`
        at depth 0). User space can change the governor, the
        OPP range of each domain, the headroom, the rate limit and an
        idle-depth limit at run time (the `power` endpoint;
        `POWER.ELF serve` applies a profile); the kernel refuses a value
        below the real-time deadline floor or the WCET reference OPP and
        records the refusal. Cost, measured
        under QEMU -icount by the gate's energy row (one run per ISA and
        tracker, 2026-10-03): switch hook 73-76 instructions per call on
        riscv64 and 128-271 on aarch64; tick hook 61-71 and 187-382.
      n = none of the above is compiled. The default is y on the
      wearable, IoT/HMI, desktop and server presets (battery or power bill
      first) and n on the others (robot, industrial, automotive, firmware,
      gateway, generic: the clock stays where firmware left it).

      The energy MODE (performance / balanced / endurance) is not a Kconfig
      symbol: the signed topology chooses it (`[energy] mode`, invariant
      I5), and every built-in profile topology says `performance`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Thermal framework (zones, trips, cooling by OPP capping)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config THERMAL
    # cargo-feature: thermal
    bool "Thermal framework: zones, trips and cooling by OPP capping"
    depends on ENERGY
    depends on SOC_FRAMEWORKS || !BOARD_VF2
    # The Raspberry Pi's sensor and ARM clock are the VideoCore firmware's.
    depends on RPI_FIRMWARE || !ARCH_AARCH64
    default y if ENERGY && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED && (PRESET_ROBOT || PRESET_IOT_HMI || PRESET_GATEWAY || PRESET_DESKTOP || PRESET_SERVER)
    help
      Emits the kernel cargo feature `thermal` (tools/build/kconfig_to_cargo.py).
      crates/core/energy (thermal, opp) decides, crates/drivers/thermal
      reads the sensors and moves the clocks: the DTB's `thermal-zones`
      become zones with trips; a hot trip caps the performance domain by OPP
      steps, never below the real-time deadline floor or the WCET safety
      floor; what the clock cannot do (a floor holds it up, the clock will
      not move, the sensor is lost) raises the degrade level, and a critical
      trip requests an orderly shutdown. A lost sensor caps its domains at
      the deepest state of the zone's cooling maps.

      Board support: Raspberry Pi (temperature and ARM clock through the
      VideoCore mailbox, `brcm,bcm2835-mbox`), StarFive JH7110 (sfctemp,
      with the CPU clock of the SoC frameworks, hence the dependency on
      SOC_FRAMEWORKS there) and SpacemiT K1 (the five-channel TSEN sensor,
      started at probe and refused when its window does not hold the start
      sequence; with SOC_K1, each cluster's clock and its supply move
      together: the P1 PMIC buck over the I2C master, the voltage of each
      OPP from the device tree's `opp-microvolt`, the rail before the clock
      going up and the clock before the rail going down, a buck two clusters
      share held at the higher one's need). A K1 voltage step is several I2C
      transfers (hundreds of microseconds at 400 kHz), more than a spin lock
      may be held, so the K1's tick holds no lock across it: the runtime is
      taken out of the thermal state for the tick, the SoC frameworks' lock is
      taken for each clock operation alone, and the transfers sleep between
      controller steps. It needs an energy model with the K1's OPP
      frequencies (the Linux trees carry no opp-microwatt: a signed topology
      or a tree that gives it). x86_64 has the package sensor (Intel
      coretemp MSRs, AMD Zen over SMN), probed from CPUID before any register
      is read and refused under a hypervisor (QEMU has none); its zone has no
      device tree, so its trips are THERMAL_X86_PASSIVE_C, THERMAL_X86_HOT_C
      and THERMAL_X86_CRITICAL_C. Its clock is the CPU's own level request,
      Intel HWP or AMD CPPC (crates/drivers/thermal/src/x86.rs), behind the
      CPUID bit that says the CPU has it (there is no fixup for a rdmsr that
      faults): the energy model's OPP is laid on the CPU's own range of
      levels, and a CPU that takes no request, or whose firmware keeps the
      control, refuses every cap, so heat there raises the degrade level and
      requests the shutdown at the critical trip instead of capping a clock.
      RAPL's package energy (Intel since Sandy Bridge, AMD Zen) is sampled
      each tick and shown in the introspection tree's `thermal/`. The level
      request is made for an
      OPP of the energy model, so it reaches a CPU only when the model has a
      domain with two OPPs (the topology's [energy.domain.*]); the sensor,
      the degrade level, the critical shutdown and the energy counter work
      either way.

      Wired: at boot the kernel builds the board's runtime from the DTB's
      thermal zones over the energy model (kernel/src/boot/thermal.rs) and
      creates a `thermal` task on a CPU outside RCU_NOCBS_CPUS and the
      topology's isolcpus. It wakes at the zones' polling delay (the passive
      one while a trip is active), asks the energy governor seam what each
      domain wants (the OPP the governor recorded; none while the governor is
      Fixed) and the real-time density admitted on the domain's most loaded
      hart, ticks the runtime, and leaves every trip, floor-bound domain,
      level change, lost sensor and shutdown request as a console line and a
      `thermal` decision record (`why list`). The introspection tree's
      `thermal/` shows the state. The task is the governor's apply step
      (ENERGY), which this option gives zones: without THERMAL, or on a
      board whose tree declares none, the same task applies the governor's
      choices with no zone. A board with no sensor (QEMU, an x86_64 machine
      whose probe finds none) builds no zone; with no clock to move either,
      no task: nothing reads a sensor and no clock is touched (invariant
      I4). Not wired yet: the level reaches no consumer (the power
      service has no channel for a level the kernel raised); `thermal/` and
      the console are where it shows. Not for a small board
      (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED), which keeps its own native handling.
      n = not linked.

config THERMAL_TASK_PRIORITY
    int "Thermal task priority (outside the RT band: 12..30)"
    range 12 30
    default 20
    help
      Read only with ENERGY. kernel/src/boot/thermal.rs, `thermal`: the task that reads the zones,
      caps the clocks and applies the energy governor's choices (with ENERGY
      alone, on a board with a clock to move, it only applies them). Never real-time, and pinned to a CPU outside
      RCU_NOCBS_CPUS and the topology's isolcpus, so a real-time task never
      waits for it and it never touches a timer a real-time task owns. A
      smaller number runs it sooner under CPU load; it should run before the
      reclaim task (MM_RECLAIM_PRIORITY, 24 by default): a machine that is too
      hot has no use for freed pages.

      Cost: one stack, and per wake-up one sensor read (a register read on the
      JH7110, a mailbox round trip on a Pi) per zone that is due.

config THERMAL_X86_PASSIVE_C
    int "x86_64 package: passive trip (degrees Celsius)"
    range 40 119
    default 75
    depends on THERMAL && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Read only with THERMAL on x86_64. kernel/src/boot/thermal.rs builds the
      machine's one zone, the package, from the sensor the CPUID probe found
      (Intel coretemp, AMD Zen over SMN): an x86_64 machine has no device tree
      and the signed topology declares no thermal zones, so its trips are
      this and the two below. At the `passive` trip every performance domain
      of the energy model that has two OPPs is capped one OPP per read, down
      to its deepest and never below the real-time deadline floor or the WCET
      safety floor; the cap is lifted a step per read once the package is
      under it. Must be below THERMAL_X86_HOT_C (the kernel refuses to build
      the zone otherwise).

config THERMAL_X86_HOT_C
    int "x86_64 package: hot trip (degrees Celsius)"
    range 50 120
    default 85
    depends on THERMAL && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Read only with THERMAL on x86_64, as THERMAL_X86_PASSIVE_C. A `hot`
      trip raises the degrade level to 2, slow. Must be below
      THERMAL_X86_CRITICAL_C (the kernel refuses to build the zone otherwise).
      Intel reports against TjMax (usually 100), AMD as Tctl (Zen 1 parts are
      offset from the die by up to 27 C, which the sensor subtracts).

config THERMAL_X86_CRITICAL_C
    int "x86_64 package: critical trip (degrees Celsius)"
    range 60 130
    default 100
    depends on THERMAL && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Read only with THERMAL on x86_64, as THERMAL_X86_HOT_C. A `critical`
      trip sets the degrade level to 3, contained, and requests an orderly
      shutdown. Keep it under the part's own THERMTRIP, which cuts power
      without one.

config THERMAL_X86_POLL_MS
    int "x86_64 package: polling delay (ms)"
    range 100 60000
    default 1000
    depends on THERMAL && ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Read only with THERMAL on x86_64: the thermal task reads the package
      sensor this often (an MSR read on Intel; on AMD two config-space port
      accesses under the port lock, which every PCI config access shares).

choice
    prompt "Utilisation tracker (with ENERGY)"
    default ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW
    help
      How ENERGY ages the utilisation signals. Both are integer and do O(1)
      work per update whatever the gap since the last one. Surfaces as the
      bool consts azos_limits::ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW / ENERGY_UTIL_PELT.
      No effect without ENERGY.

    config ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW
        bool "Window (WALT-like): max(last window, mean of the last four)"
        help
          Busy time per ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW_US window; the signal is the
          larger of the last closed window and the mean of the last four.
          A step to fully busy shows after one window; a task that stops
          reads zero four windows later.

    config ENERGY_UTIL_PELT
        bool "PELT-like geometric decay, half-life 32 periods"
        help
          avg = avg*y + input*(1-y) per ENERGY_PELT_PERIOD_US period, with
          y^32 = 1/2, in Q32 fixed point.

endchoice

config ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW_US
    int "Utilisation window (us, window tracker)"
    range 1000 100000
    default 10000
    help
      Length of one window of the window tracker. No effect without ENERGY
      or with ENERGY_UTIL_PELT.

config ENERGY_PELT_PERIOD_US
    int "Utilisation period (us, PELT-like tracker)"
    range 256 10000
    default 1024
    help
      Length of one decay step of the PELT-like tracker; the half-life is 32
      of them (32.8 ms at the default). No effect without ENERGY or with
      ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW.

config ENERGY_BATTERY_RESERVE_PCT
    int "Battery reserve (percent of charge)"
    range 1 50
    default 10
    help
      The charge at and below which the kernel holds every performance
      domain at its lowest legal OPP, whatever the power service's policy
      asks (the deadline and safety floors still win): what is left is for the
      safe stop (landing, parking the actuators). Neither the signed topology
      nor the power service can lower it. Only a machine whose topology
      declares `[degrade.NAME]` rows has a battery for it to apply to. No
      effect without ENERGY. A product sets it per image (its defconfig).

      The default is a placeholder, not a derived number: 10 is where the
      INA219 driver's failsafe already lands (`FAILSAFE_LAND_PCT`); no
      discharge model of a pack and no measured safe stop supports it. A
      product that needs another has to measure the energy of its safe stop.

config ENERGY_BATTERY_REPORT_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "Battery report timeout (ms)"
    range 3000 3600000
    default 10000
    help
      The oldest the power service's last accepted battery report may be. A
      service that died or stalled leaves its last charge in force, and the
      kernel cannot tell a full pack from an empty one by it: past this age the
      kernel holds the battery reserve (lowest legal OPP, floors still win)
      until the next accepted report. Counted from boot, so a service that
      never starts is held the same way. The service reports every 2 s
      (SERVE_PERIOD_MS in userspace/services/power) and the kernel may fire
      its sleep late, so the least is 3 s. Only a machine whose topology
      declares `[degrade.NAME]` rows has a report to age. No effect without
      ENERGY.
